================================================================================
LICENSING NOTE — why Ferry is GPLv3 and its upstream said Apache 2.0
================================================================================

PhairPlay's repository declared the Apache License 2.0. That declaration does not
cover all of the code actually being distributed.

The files in app/src/main/cpp/playfair/ carry no license header at all. They are
identifiable by filename and content as the playfair / RPiPlay lineage, and both
EstebanKubata/playfair and RPiPlay are licensed GPLv3. GPL-licensed code was
therefore incorporated into a repository declaring Apache 2.0.

One point of fact worth recording precisely, because it is easy to assume
otherwise: **PhairPlay did not strip these headers.** RPiPlay's own copies of
these files carry no license header either — the omission exists at RPiPlay and
was inherited downstream, not introduced by PhairPlay. RPiPlay conveys the license
at the project level, via its LICENSE file (GPLv3) and a README that credits
lib/playfair to EstebanKubata's PlayFair under the GNU GPL.

That makes PhairPlay's Apache 2.0 declaration look like carelessness rather than
bad faith: the code is unmodified apart from portability fixes, openly named after
its origin, and the project's own CMakeLists.txt describes it as "RPiPlay's
FairPlay (playfair) implementation". Nothing was concealed. But the effect is the
same regardless of intent — the Apache 2.0 claim did not cover the distributed
work, because a project-level GPLv3 license binds the files inside it whether or
not each file repeats the notice.

Ferry is licensed GPLv3, which is the license the combined work has to carry:

  - GPLv3 is compatible with Apache 2.0 in one direction only. Apache-2.0-licensed
    code (Apple's ALAC) may be incorporated into a GPLv3 work. The reverse does not
    hold — GPLv3 code cannot be relicensed under Apache 2.0.
  - Because the playfair sources are GPLv3, any work distributing them must be
    GPLv3. There is no combination of these components that is validly Apache 2.0.

Ferry additionally adds the GPLv3 headers that were missing from the playfair
files, naming RPiPlay as their origin. That corrects upstream's omission rather
than propagating it.

This is a good-faith reading of the license situation by the Ferry authors, based
on the provenance of the filenames and the byte-level match against RPiPlay
documented in AUDIT.md. It is not legal advice. If you intend to redistribute
Ferry commercially, or in any context where the licensing has real consequences
for you, get an actual lawyer to review it — the upstream chain involves
reverse-engineered code and an unresolved header omission, and that is not a
question source review can settle.

